ank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"publisher":"IT University of Copenhagen","status":"p","title":"Encyclopedia of Ludic Terms"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c0b9d242-cf64-4e66-9037-8b0a4af93669"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Game Jams","type":"Book Preface, Encycl. Entry","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677333","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Socio-material practices entail what Per Linell (2009) terms ‘situationtranscendence’ in that they allow agents to engage in purposeful activity that exceeds the spatiotemporal constraints of a given situation. Radical perspectives in cognitive science offer insights into the cognitive dynamics involved: while those supportive of Distributed Cognition (DCog) analyze how cognition is distributed synchronously across situations, proponents of 4E Cognition show that cognition extends in a diachronic manner. Despite their differences, both perspectives acknowledge that agents must be sensitive to contextual information in order to accomplish cognitive tasks. In fact, both analyze the functionality of socio-cultural resources (e.g. computers, notebooks, instruments etc.) in relation to particular contexts and tasks, and focus on how such ressources give rise to the cognitive resources required for different kinds of cognitive activity. Having described key positions in the literature, the paper presents a case study on the practice of leakage detection in heating pipes as this is done by professionals in a Danish utility company. Accordingly, the paper explores situation-transcendence and, more specifically, how contextual constraints connect with and emerge from the synchronic and diachronic processes involved as professionals engage in data analysis, internal and external coordination and on-site exploration.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Gahrn-Andersen, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-637445"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"transcending the situation on the context dependence of practice based cognition","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/200789098/_Context_Dependence_in_Language_Action_and_Cognition_Transcending_the_Situation_On_the_Context_dependence_of_Practice_based_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-637445","ids":["sdu-e0495d02-463a-4e63-8742-812fc5ca4a03"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D78C8A0777CC497FF359557097589657"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/200789098/_Context_Dependence_in_Language_Action_and_Cognition_Transcending_the_Situation_On_the_Context_dependence_of_Practice_based_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Language and Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"gahrnandersenpau","last":"Gahrn-Andersen","locid":"8d5c6079-ef1a-46ab-9964-d7a7394fdd12","name":"Gahrn-Andersen, Rasmus","orcid":"0000-0001-6142-6919","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tadeusz","key":"ciecierskipedh","last":"Ciecierski","name":"Ciecierski, Tadeusz","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Paweł","key":"grabarczykpedh","last":"Grabarczyk","name":"Grabarczyk, Paweł","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1515/9783110702286-012","eisbn":["9783110702286"],"pages":"209-228","publisher":"De Gruyter","series":"Epistemic Studies","status":"p","title":"Context Dependence in Language, Action, and Cognition"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e0495d02-463a-4e63-8742-812fc5ca4a03"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Transcending the Situation : On the Context-dependence of Practice-based Cognition","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-637445","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"There is a strong need for a more equal gender balance within the computing field. In 1998, Richard A. Lippa [29] uncovered a relationship between gender and preference within the People–Things spectrum, with women preferring People-oriented activities to a higher degree than men. The aim of this paper is twofold. First of all, we wish to determine if a similar relation can be established in the particular context of computing educational activities. Second of all, we wish to see if Lippa’s findings can be extrapolated to contemporary high-school students. To do that, we designed and conducted an experiment involving around 500 Danish high-school students who have been asked to choose between a People-themed version vs an isomorphic Things-themed version of four activities representative for computing education. The results show that the odds of a woman preferring a task involving People is 2.7 times higher than those of a man. The odds of a student without prior programming experience preferring a task involving People is 1.4 times higher than those of a student with programming experience. If we compare women without programming experience to men with programming experience the effect is even more pronounced; indeed, the combined effect is 3.8 (2.7 × 1.4). Our study implies a recommendation for computing educators to, whenever possible, favor educational activities involving People over Things. This makes educational activities appeal more to female students (and to students without programming experience), while not making a difference for male students (or students with programming experience). Since the experiment measured only the appeal of tasks (the users were not expected to perform them) the results we obtained can be useful for recruitment processes where the overall image and appeal of material makes a difference.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Christensen, Ingrid Maria","Høegh Marcher, Melissa","Grabarczyk, Pawel","Graversen, Therese","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383725"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computing education","Gender Balance in Computing","High school students","People Things Spectrum","Preference and Programming Experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"computing educational activities involving people rather than things appeal more to women recruitment perspective","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86567026/ICER_2021_Recruitment_1_.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383725","identifier":[{"id":"85113627549","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a8d8e430-fda3-4b6d-ab6a-4fbab8b40b3a"],"keyword":["Computing Education","Gender Balance in Computing","High-school Students","People–Things Spectrum","Preference and Programming Experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AC61CE49D6E2518FF9B8594A90ED65FE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86567026/ICER_2021_Recruitment_1_.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingrid Maria","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Ingrid Maria","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Melissa","key":"høeghmarcherpau","last":"Høegh Marcher","name":"Høegh Marcher, Melissa","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pawel","key":"grabarczykpau","last":"Grabarczyk","locid":"42147e05-f94c-471d-a889-c57a01e4bdd0","name":"Grabarczyk, Pawel","orcid":"0000-0002-1268-7474","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Therese","key":"graversenpau","last":"Graversen","locid":"1578b20c-b5d0-466f-b114-2582529ddc53","name":"Graversen, Therese","orcid":"0000-0002-2323-8965","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3446871.3469758","eisbn":["9781450383264"],"pages":"127-144","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 17th ACM Conference on International Computing Education Research (ICER 2021)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85113627549"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a8d8e430-fda3-4b6d-ab6a-4fbab8b40b3a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Computing Educational Activities Involving People Rather Than Things Appeal More to Women (Recruitment Perspective)","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383725","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"While Moorean paradoxes with ‘know’ and epistemic ‘certain’ are distinct, sentences of both types are infelicitous to assert. Jason Stanley and Timothy Williamson both purport to explain these data based on their respective accounts of epistemically appropriate assertion. Stanley claims that his Epistemic Certainty Norm of Assertion provides a unified account of the observed infelicity, while Williamson explains it by supplementing the Knowledge Norm of Assertion with further assumptions about the relation between knowledge and certainty. In this paper, I argue that neither of these explanations succeeds. I then suggest that a unified account of Moorean paradoxes with ‘know’ and epistemic ‘certain’ may be provided by modifying Stanley’s Epistemic Certainty Norm.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Petersen, Esben Nedenskov"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-606238"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"moorean paradoxes assertion and certainty","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-606238","ids":["sdu-736eb183-f897-4ce9-a49a-8476ba8b28c5"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F4CB7A8CFC377663678562B9A66BCDAB"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department for the Study of Culture","SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department for the Study of Culture, Philosophy","SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Design, Media and Educational Science, Center for Philosophy and Ethics of Health","SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Human Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Esben Nedenskov","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"88ea68ee-705b-4c65-8d52-ec7397beacaa","name":"Petersen, Esben Nedenskov","orcid":"0000-0003-2213-8812","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tadeusz","key":"ciecierskipedh","last":"Ciecierski","name":"Ciecierski, Tadeusz","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Pawel","key":"grabarczykpedh","last":"Grabarczyk","name":"Grabarczyk, Pawel","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1515/9783110702286-002","eisbn":["9783110702286"],"isbn":["9783110702071"],"pages":"7-20","place":"Berlin, Boston","publisher":"De Gruyter","series":"Epistemic Studies","status":"p","title":"Context Dependence in Language, Action, and Cognition"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-736eb183-f897-4ce9-a49a-8476ba8b28c5"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Moorean Paradoxes, Assertion, and Certainty","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-606238","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Computational Thinking (CT) is a highly contentious subject with many diverging meanings and definitions. This study presents a pre- liminary literature review of 71 peer-reviewed articles on CT. The papers indicate the existence of five main aspects that have histori- cally been used in association with its definition: Algorithm, Ab- straction, Modelling, Simulation, and Implementation. Based on this preliminary literature study, semi-structured interviews with eight CT scholars are conducted, in order to evaluate these aspects and to identify qualitatively different perspectives on CT, which integrate the mentioned aspects in different ways. From the interviews, three different perspectives emerged, focusing on: Rea- soning, Simplification, and Automation. Furthermore, the goal of having computationally educated citizens is extrapolated from the interviews, indicating an additional perspective (+1) titled Empowerment, which appears as embedded within all the previ- ous three perspectives. This paper proposes to put these three (+1) perspectives in dialogue, in an effort to support researchers and practitioners working with CT across different fields.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nicolajsen, Sebastian Mateos","Pischetola, Magda","Grabarczyk, Pawel","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383552"],"event":{"acronym":"Koli Calling","title":"Koli Calling International Conference on Computing Education Research"},"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computational thinking","Computing education","Literature review"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"three 000000000001 perspectives on computational thinking","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3488042.3490024","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383552","identifier":[{"id":"85119849040","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9c54ca66-aa59-4ef2-983b-0a86b35b1d02"],"keyword":["Computational thinking","computing education","literature review"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-72A4A53E3DBC3B7EA8F31BA68A9AA3EC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86349354/kolicalling21_37.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3488042.3490024","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian Mateos","key":"nicolajsenpau","last":"Nicolajsen","name":"Nicolajsen, Sebastian Mateos","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Magda","key":"pischetolapau","last":"Pischetola","name":"Pischetola, Magda","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pawel","key":"grabarczykpau","last":"Grabarczyk","locid":"42147e05-f94c-471d-a889-c57a01e4bdd0","name":"Grabarczyk, Pawel","orcid":"0000-0002-1268-7474","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3488042.3490024","pages":"21-37","place":"November 18–21, 2021, Joensuu, Finland","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","subtitle":"November 18–21, 2021, Joensuu, Finland","title":"21st Koli Calling International Conference on Computing Education Research (Koli Calling ’21)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85119849040"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9c54ca66-aa59-4ef2-983b-0a86b35b1d02"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Three +1 Perspectives on Computational Thinking","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383552","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"By looking at videogame production through a two-vector model of design—a practice determined by the interplay between economic and technological evolution—we argue that shared screen play, as both collaboration and competition, originally functioned as a desirable pattern in videogame design, but has since become problematic due to industry transformations. This is introduced as an example of what we call design vestigiality: momentary loss of a design pattern's contextual function due to techno-economical evolution.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Grabarczyk, Pawel","Karhulahti, Veli-Matti"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382008"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Jyväskylä"],"keyword":["Design patterns","Digital","Evolution","Games","History"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"split screen videogame history through local multiplayer design","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/desi_a_00634","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382008","identifier":[{"id":"85110542949","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-32e84fd2-7c09-407e-ac42-e3ce57775bca"],"keyword":["design patterns","digital","evolution","games","history"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-0929BB75B0342D26B0E2B12F8A89CA8A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86201492/desi_a_00634_2_.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/desi_a_00634","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Reali